---
/**
 * Refund Policy. Draft. Needs a human read before launch.
 *
 * The terms on this page are a business decision, not a template: a $150
 * deposit that stops being refundable once work starts, a balance that is
 * simply not charged if the client leaves before launch, and a month to month
 * retainer with fourteen days notice. Do not soften or invent around these
 * without asking, because they are what the FAQ on the homepage promises too.
 */
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { business } from "../config/business";
import { plans } from "../content/pricing";

const [website, retainer] = plans;
---

<Legal
  title={`Refund policy: ${business.name}`}
  description="What happens to your money if you cancel a website build or stop the monthly retainer, including how the deposit is treated and the notice period."
  path="/refunds"
  heading="Refund policy"
  standfirst="What happens to your money if you change your mind. Written so you can work out where you stand without emailing us."
>
  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A website is {website.amount}, paid as $150 to start and $150 when it
      goes live. The first $150 is not refundable once we begin work. The
      second $150 is never charged if you cancel before launch.
    </li>
    <li>
      The {retainer.amount} a month retainer runs month to month. Give us
      fourteen days notice and it stops. The month you are in is not refunded.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>The rest of this page is the detail behind those two lines.</p>

  <h2>The website build</h2>

  <h3>The deposit</h3>
  <p>
    The $150 deposit is what lets us start. Once we have it we begin writing
    your text, which is the bulk of the job and happens before you see
    anything. That work cannot be given back, so the deposit is not refundable
    once we have started.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is one exception. If you pay the deposit and then cancel before we
    have started work, we refund it in full. In practice that means telling us
    within two business days, because we usually start the next morning. Ask
    and we will tell you honestly whether we have begun.
  </p>

  <h3>The balance</h3>
  <p>
    The remaining $150 is only invoiced on the day the site goes live. If you
    cancel at any point before then, for any reason or none, you are not
    charged it. There is nothing to claim back because it was never taken.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the part worth understanding: the most you can lose by hiring us
    and changing your mind is $150.
  </p>

  <h3>If you cancel mid build</h3>
  <p>
    Tell us and we will stop. You keep the deposit position described above,
    you are not invoiced for the balance, and we will send you whatever we have
    written so far so it is not wasted. We will not hold finished text hostage
    over a cancelled job.
  </p>

  <h3>If we cancel</h3>
  <p>
    If we walk away from a job for our own reasons, or we cannot finish it, we
    refund the deposit in full even if we have done the work. That is our
    problem, not yours. If we cancel because we cannot get the details or
    photographs we need after sixty days of asking, the deposit stays with us
    and the balance is not charged.
  </p>

  <h3>If the site is broken</h3>
  <p>
    For thirty days after launch we fix anything broken that is our fault, free
    and without argument. That is a repair, not a refund. If we cannot get it
    working, we will refund what you paid for the site.
  </p>

  <h2>The monthly retainer</h2>

  <h3>Cancelling</h3>
  <p>
    The retainer is month to month. There is no minimum term and no annual
    contract. To stop it, email us at least fourteen days before your next
    billing date and it will not be charged again.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fourteen days rather than none because posts are written and scheduled
    ahead of time. When you give notice we finish what is already written and
    hand it to you, so you are not paying for work you never see.
  </p>

  <h3>The month you are in</h3>
  <p>
    The current month is not refunded, whole or in part. That month's work is
    already commissioned and mostly already done by the time notice arrives.
  </p>

  <h3>If you cancel with less than fourteen days notice</h3>
  <p>
    The next month is charged and then it stops. You get that month's full
    eight posts and ad creative. We will not bill a month and then not do the
    work.
  </p>

  <h3>If we drop the ball</h3>
  <p>
    If we miss the volume we promised in a month, which is eight posts and one
    round of new ad creative, tell us. We will either make it up in the
    following month or refund that month, your choice.
  </p>

  <h2>How a refund is paid</h2>
  <p>
    Back to the card or account you paid from, within ten business days of us
    agreeing it. We do not issue credit notes and we will not push you into
    taking store credit instead of your money.
  </p>

  <h2>How to ask</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href={`mailto:${business.email}`}>{business.email}</a> and say what
    you want to cancel. You do not need a reason. We will reply within two
    business days confirming what happens to the money, in writing, before
    anything is processed.
  </p>

  <h2>What this page does not change</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing here removes any right you have under {business.state} law,
    including under the Texas Deceptive Trade Practices Act. If this page and
    the law disagree, the law wins.
  </p>
</Legal>
